    "title": "The arras contract decides what you bought",
    "source_article": "property-contracts.txt, buying-10-steps.txt, bank-guarantees.txt",
    "post_text": "The arras draft usually lands in your inbox two days before signing day. That window isn't a courtesy. It's your only real chance to change the contract.\n\nMost buyers in Spain check the price and skim the rest, which is a shame, because the arras decides what you actually bought.\n\nThe agency normally adapts a standard contract, filling in the property details as they appear on the Nota Simple. Fine so far. But beyond price and description, it should pin down the completion date, usually two to three months after signing so your documents and mortgage have time. It should spell out the penalties, and they cut both ways: pull out and you lose your 10 percent deposit, but if the seller pulls out, they pay you double. And it should capture the specifics. Which furniture stays. Which repairs the seller covers. Whether appliances are included, and whether water and electricity will actually be on.\n\nIf it's not written in the arras, it was never agreed. That's the whole game.\n\nThe money deserves a thought too. That 10 percent often goes straight to the owners, which is common in Spain, but a safeguarded account at a financial institution is safer. Ask for it.\n\nBuying a new build? Then you'll also want completion date clauses and the bank guarantee that protects staged payments.\n\nSo read the draft the day it arrives, line by line, and send your amendments before signing day. At the table it's too late.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #MoveToSpain",
