    "title": "Spain taxes your empty holiday home",
    "source_article": "irnr.txt, non-resident-property-tax.txt",
    "post_text": "Your Spanish holiday home earned nothing this year. Spain will still tax you on it, and no bill ever arrives to remind you. That's the trap.\n\nStart by working out which side of the line you're on, because Spain treats you as a tax resident if any of these is true. You spent more than 183 days in Spain this calendar year, continuous or not. Your spouse and underage children live there. Or the majority of your income and economic activity is in Spain.\n\nNone of those? Then you're a non-resident, and IRNR applies simply because you own property. Spain imputes income at 1.1 percent of the cadastral value, or 2 percent in municipalities without a recent cadastral revision, then taxes that at 19 percent if you live in the EU or EEA and 24 percent for everyone else.\n\nPut real numbers on it. On a 300,000 euro property at 1.1 percent, an owner in Norway pays 19 percent of 3,300 euros, which works out to about 627 euros a year.\n\nThe filing is Modelo 210, due by 31 December, and it always covers the previous year. Rent the place out and the rhythm changes to quarterly.\n\nIgnore it long enough and things escalate for real: fines, closure of bank accounts, even confiscation of property in extreme cases.\n\nSo this week, count your days in Spain and check whether last year's Modelo 210 was actually filed. Plenty of owners assume someone else did it.\n\nGeneral information, not tax advice.\n\n#SpanishTax #Modelo210 #HolidayHome",
