    "title": "The 48-hour squatter rule",
    "source_article": "squatter-laws.txt",
    "post_text": "You have 48 hours to get squatters out of your Spanish home without a judge. Miss that window and you could be looking at two years.\n\nSounds dramatic, I know. But the clock is real, and it starts the moment someone enters.\n\nInside those first 48 hours, the police can remove occupiers on the spot. All you need is proof the place is yours. After that? You're in court, and Spanish courts move slowly here for a reason.\n\nThe law actually splits squatting in two. Breaking into an inhabited home is allanamiento de morada, and it's treated seriously. Occupying an \"uninhabited\" one, okupacion, is much softer. Guess which category your empty holiday home falls into. Add Article 47 of the Constitution, which promises everyone decent housing, and you can see why evictions drag: 18 months to 2 years in some regions, though many resolve faster.\n\nIn court you'll be proving you actually use the place: deeds, photos, flight tickets, even neighbours vouching for you. Meanwhile the squatters get to argue their side.\n\nAnd whatever your instincts say, don't change the locks, don't block re-entry, don't confront anyone. Spanish law can flip all of that against you.\n\nSo the real protection isn't legal. It's speed. A police-connected alarm timestamps the entry and keeps you inside your 48 hours. So does a neighbour with a key. Some insurers even offer illegal occupation cover. Set the alarm and the neighbour up before the empty season, not after the phone call.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #SpanishHomeOwners #HolidayHome",
