    "title": "Cash in Spain now needs paperwork",
    "source_article": "cash-rules.txt",
    "post_text": "Want to take 3,000 euros in cash out of your Spanish bank tomorrow? Then you needed to tell the tax office today. I'm not exaggerating.\n\nSince 2025, withdrawals above 3,000 euros, or any amount involving 500 euro notes, have to be reported to the Agencia Tributaria 24 hours in advance. You notify via their website with proof of ID, sometimes proof of where the funds came from, and you get a certificate to show the bank. From 100,000 euros the notice period stretches to 72 hours.\n\nPaying in cash has ceilings of its own: 1,000 euros for any transaction involving a business, 1,000 between private individuals, and 10,000 if you're a non-resident who doesn't pay tax in Spain.\n\nEven transfers come with paperwork. Any cash transfer within Spain over 100,000 euros, property purchases included, has to be declared on the S1 form, and sending more than 10,000 euros to someone means documenting where the money came from.\n\nBreak the rules and fines run from up to 50 percent for minor infractions to 150 percent where fraud's involved. One softener: if payer or payee self-reports within three months, they may be exempt.\n\nCrossing the border with more than 10,000 euros in cash? Declare it, or risk losing up to half the undeclared amount.\n\nSo if you're buying, furnishing or renovating, plan on transfers, not envelopes, and keep receipts for large transactions for at least five years. Questions can come long after the money moved.\n\nGeneral information, not tax advice.\n\n#BankingInSpain #SpanishTax #ExpatSpain",
