    "title": "Tourist rentals need a licence",
    "source_article": "legally-renting.txt",
    "post_text": "\"We'll rent it out when we're not there.\" Sounds simple, until you learn that any rental contract under three months makes you a tourist landlord in Spain, and that status needs a licence.\n\nThe licence comes in two forms, individual for private owners and collective for companies managing several properties, and its job is to certify hygiene, safety and quality standards, and that you're paying the right taxes.\n\nGetting one means bringing the property up to standard, obtaining habitability and energy efficiency certificates, registering with the local tourism agency where required, then submitting the application at your tourist office. Expect one to six months depending on region and demand.\n\nSkipping it is where the numbers turn serious. Fines typically run 4,000 to 60,000 euros, rising sharply for repeat infractions, and up to 111,000 euros in Ibiza. Catalunya has dedicated teams hunting illegal lets. Barcelona has made short-term private room rentals illegal, and new licences for entire homes are now very hard to come by. Valencia mostly issues them to ground-floor properties in popular areas.\n\nThere's an upside hiding in all this, though. A property that already has a licence attached is a genuine selling point. And renting legally is what makes proper rental insurance valid, and what lets you list on the big booking platforms at all.\n\nSo if rental income is part of your purchase maths, check licence availability in that exact area before you buy. Two streets apart can be two different answers.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#HolidayRental #SpainProperty #HolidayHome",
