    "title": "The Nota Simple line buyers never read",
    "source_article": "nota-simple.txt, land-registry.txt, buying-10-steps.txt",
    "post_text": "There's a line on a 10 euro document that can quietly shrink your Spanish mortgage, and most buyers never read it.\n\nThe document is the Nota Simple, the Land Registry's summary of a property: current owner, mortgages and debts, boundaries, square metres, land classification.\n\nEveryone tells you to check the debts, and they're right, because in Spain debts travel with the home, not the seller. Buy a property with unpaid charges and they become yours. The Nota Simple also confirms the seller actually owns what they're selling, and yes, there have been cases of squatters selling homes that were never theirs.\n\nBut the line I mean is the valor catastral, the official estimate of the property's value. If it's registered lower than your agreed price, the bank can base your mortgage on that lower figure. Registered 10k under your buying price? Expect a mortgage for 10k less. Which is why you want to know this before you talk to the bank, not when the offer letter arrives.\n\nGetting one is trivial. Around 10 euros online, arriving as fast as the same day, or under 4 euros in person at the registry. Anyone with a legitimate interest can request it. You'll need the property's registry identifier, which the seller or agent can supply.\n\nSo order the Nota Simple before you sign the arras, and read the cadastral value line as carefully as the debts section. It's cheap insurance either way.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #NotaSimple",
