    "title": "Fined for the advert itself: the EPC",
    "source_article": "epc.txt, lost-documents.txt",
    "post_text": "In Spain you can be fined for the advert itself. Listing a home without an energy certificate runs 300 to 600 euros, before a single viewing happens.\n\nThat's because the EPC is required at the advertising stage, not just at completion. The certificate must exist before the property is listed, a copy goes into the notarial deed when you sell, and notaries may refuse to complete a sale without one. Having no certificate at all, or falsifying one, moves the fine into the 1,000 to 6,000 euro range.\n\nHere's the good news, though. Getting one is genuinely easy. A certified technician inspects insulation, windows, heating, cooling and lighting, then issues a rating from A to G. Expect 100 to 300 euros and less than a week, and it stays valid for ten years. A decent rating can even support your asking price and open access to green funding and subsidies.\n\nOne step catches even professionals, which is that the certificate must also be registered with your regional authority. The technician usually handles this. Usually. Confirm it was done and keep the reference with your property papers, because each region runs its own inspections and penalties.\n\nAnd if you lose the certificate later? The technician who issued it can supply a copy, or a licensed assessor can reissue one for 60 to 150 euros.\n\nSelling or letting soon? Make the EPC the first document you sort, because everything else waits behind the listing.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #SellingInSpain #EPC",
